# North America batch (1820-1919): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"na182032": dict(
 bluf="Congress wanted to admit Missouri without breaking the balance of 11 free and 11 slave states in the Senate. Speaker of the House Henry Clay's settlement admitted Missouri with slavery and Maine without, and barred slavery from the rest of the Louisiana Purchase north of 36°30'. The line held the Union together for 34 years and fixed the principle that Congress would decide where slavery could spread, the question that broke the Union in 1861.",
 now="The compromise set the pattern of admitting slave and free states in pairs, which governed national politics until 1854; its repeal that year and the Dred Scott ruling of 1857 removed the last legal brake on slavery's spread.",
 story="Missouri applied for statehood in 1819 as a slave state while the Senate stood at 11 free and 11 slave states. New York Representative James Tallmadge proposed to bar new slaves from Missouri and free the children of those already there; the House passed it and the Senate refused. Speaker of the House Henry Clay brokered the settlement of 1820: Missouri entered with slavery, Maine entered free, and slavery was barred from the Louisiana Purchase north of 36°30'.",
 why="Missouri's admission with slavery would have given slave states a Senate majority. Northern congressmen wanted to stop slavery spreading into the Louisiana Purchase; Southern congressmen held that Congress had no power to set conditions on a new state.",
 then="The line held for 34 years. The Kansas-Nebraska Act of 1854 repealed it and let settlers decide, and the Dred Scott ruling of 1857 declared any such ban unconstitutional.",
 fact="Former President Thomas Jefferson wrote that the dispute had woken him 'like a fire bell in the night' and that he took it for the knell of the Union."),
"na182333": dict(
 bluf="US President James Monroe and Secretary of State John Quincy Adams wanted to keep Europe's monarchies from restoring Spain's rule over the new Latin American republics and Russia from pushing down the Pacific coast. Monroe declared the hemisphere closed to new European colonies in December 1823, with no navy able to enforce it. The claim became the charter for US primacy in the Americas and, after 1904, for a century of interventions.",
 now="It is still the basis of US policy that treats the Americas as its sphere of influence, and US officials have revived its language in response to Chinese and Russian activity in the region.",
 story="Spain's American colonies had won independence by 1823, and a French army had restored absolute monarchy in Spain itself that year. British Foreign Secretary George Canning proposed a joint Anglo-American declaration against reconquest; Secretary of State John Quincy Adams argued for a US statement alone. President James Monroe put it in his annual message to Congress on 2 December 1823: the Americas were closed to new European colonisation, and the United States would stay out of Europe's wars.",
 why="Fear that France or the Holy Alliance would help Spain reconquer its colonies came first. Russia's claim of 1821 to the Pacific coast down to 51 degrees north added a second front. Britain wanted Spain out of American trade, and its navy made the declaration enforceable.",
 then="President Theodore Roosevelt's corollary of 1904 claimed a right to police Latin American states, and it was used to justify occupations of Haiti, the Dominican Republic and Nicaragua. Latin American governments came to read the doctrine as a licence for US intervention rather than a shield against Europe.",
 fact="The United States had no navy able to enforce it. It worked because Britain's Royal Navy also wanted Spain and France kept out."),
"na182534": dict(
 bluf="New York Governor DeWitt Clinton wanted the trade of the Midwest for New York after Congress refused to pay for a canal. The state dug 584 km from the Hudson to Lake Erie in eight years and cut freight costs between the Great Lakes and New York by about 90 per cent. New York City became the country's commercial capital, and the Midwest's trade ran east to the Northeast rather than down the Mississippi to the South, a tie that held in 1861.",
 now="The canal tied the Midwest's economy to New York rather than New Orleans, which made New York the country's financial centre and aligned the Northwest with the Northeast when the Union split in 1861.",
 story="Congress refused federal money for the canal in 1817, and New York Governor DeWitt Clinton persuaded the state legislature to pay. Work began at Rome, New York, in July 1817, mostly by hand. The 584 km canal linked the Hudson River at Albany with Lake Erie at Buffalo and opened in October 1825. Freight costs between the Great Lakes and New York fell by about 90 per cent.",
 why="The Appalachians cut the Midwest's farms off from the Atlantic ports, and overland freight cost more than the goods it carried. New York stood to take the trade from Philadelphia, Baltimore and New Orleans.",
 then="Midwestern grain flowed east through New York, and Rochester, Syracuse and Buffalo grew along the route. Tolls repaid the cost of construction, and other states borrowed for canals of their own, several of which failed in the panic of 1837.",
 fact="Critics called it 'Clinton's Ditch'. Clinton opened it by pouring a keg of Lake Erie water into New York harbour, the 'wedding of the waters'."),
"na183035": dict(
 bluf="US President Andrew Jackson and the Southern states wanted the Cherokee, Creek, Choctaw, Chickasaw and Seminole off lands prized for cotton and, in Georgia, gold. Congress authorised removal by treaty in 1830, and the treaties were obtained by pressure, fraud and force. Dispossession became federal policy, the Supreme Court's recognition of tribal sovereignty in 1832 went unenforced, and about 60,000 people were moved west in the 1830s.",
 now="Removal made dispossession federal policy and set the precedent that treaty rights yield to settler demand; the term ethnic cleansing is now widely applied to it, and it underlies today's disputes over Native sovereignty and land.",
 story="Georgia extended its laws over Cherokee territory in 1828 after gold was found there, and the other Southern states followed. US President Andrew Jackson asked Congress for a removal law. The Indian Removal Act passed the House by 102 votes to 97 and Jackson signed it on 28 May 1830. It authorised the president to exchange land west of the Mississippi for tribal lands in the East by treaty. The Choctaw signed first, at Dancing Rabbit Creek in September 1830.",
 why="Cotton planters wanted the Black Belt lands of Georgia, Alabama and Mississippi. Gold in Cherokee Georgia brought thousands of prospectors. Jackson and the Southern states denied that tribes could be sovereign nations within state borders.",
 then="About 60,000 Cherokee, Creek, Choctaw, Chickasaw and Seminole people were removed in the 1830s, and thousands died on the journeys, most notoriously on the Cherokee 'Trail of Tears' of 1838. The Seminole fought removal in Florida for seven years from 1835.",
 fact="Tennessee Congressman Davy Crockett voted against the act and lost his seat the next year; he died at the Alamo six years later."),
"d-worcester": dict(
 bluf="Georgia wanted Cherokee land and had extended its laws over the Cherokee Nation; the Cherokee and their missionary allies went to the Supreme Court to stop it. Chief Justice John Marshall ruled that the Cherokee were a distinct political community in which Georgia's laws had no force. Georgia ignored the ruling and President Andrew Jackson declined to enforce it, which showed that a court's judgment binds only where the executive will act; the Cherokee were removed by force six years later.",
 now="The ruling remains a foundation of tribal sovereignty in US law and is still cited in cases over tribal jurisdiction.",
 story="Georgia had extended its laws over Cherokee territory after gold was found there and required white residents to hold a state licence. Missionary Samuel Worcester refused, was convicted and was sentenced to four years' hard labour. On 3 March 1832 Chief Justice John Marshall ruled for the Supreme Court that the Cherokee Nation was a distinct community in which Georgia's laws had no force, and that relations with it belonged to the federal government alone. Georgia refused to release Worcester.",
 why="Georgia wanted the land and the gold, and held that no sovereign nation could exist inside a state. The Cherokee had adopted a written constitution in 1827 and chose law over war.",
 then="Worcester was released in 1833 in exchange for dropping further appeals. In December 1835 a faction of about 20 Cherokee signed the Treaty of New Echota ceding all the nation's eastern land, over the protest of Principal Chief John Ross and a petition signed by more than 15,000 Cherokee. The treaty was used to force the whole nation west in 1838.",
 myth="Jackson said 'John Marshall has made his decision; now let him enforce it'. The line first appears decades later; what he did was decline to act, which had the same effect.",
 fact="Worcester had helped Cherokee editor Elias Boudinot set up the Cherokee Phoenix, the first Native American newspaper, in 1828."),
"d-alamo": dict(
 bluf="Mexican President Antonio López de Santa Anna marched north to crush a revolt by American settlers in Texas who rejected his centralised rule and Mexico's limits on slavery. He stormed the Alamo after 13 days and killed its roughly 200 defenders. The deaths gave the Texan army its battle cry, and six weeks later Santa Anna was a prisoner; an independent Texas, its annexation in 1845 and the war with Mexico of 1846 followed.",
 now="The Alamo remains a contested symbol: one reading holds it as a sacrifice for liberty, the other holds the revolt as partly a fight to keep slavery, which Mexico had abolished in 1829, and the argument shapes how Texas teaches its Mexican past.",
 story="Texan rebels had seized San Antonio in December 1835. Mexican President Antonio López de Santa Anna arrived in February 1836 and besieged the Alamo mission, held by about 200 men under Texan commanders William Travis and Jim Bowie, with former Tennessee congressman Davy Crockett among them. On 6 March 1836, after a 13-day siege, his troops stormed the walls and killed the defenders. Six weeks later, on 21 April, Texan commander Sam Houston's army defeated and captured Santa Anna at San Jacinto, shouting 'Remember the Alamo!'.",
 why="Santa Anna had scrapped Mexico's federal constitution of 1824 and centralised power, which several Mexican states resisted. The American settlers also wanted to keep slaves, which Mexican law restricted.",
 then="Texas declared independence on 2 March 1836, during the siege. Santa Anna signed the Treaties of Velasco in May recognising it, and Mexico repudiated them. The republic was annexed by the United States in 1845.",
 fact="Davy Crockett, a three-term congressman, went to Texas after losing his seat and telling his voters they could go to hell."),
"na183836": dict(
 bluf="The federal government, under President Martin Van Buren, set out to enforce the Treaty of New Echota and move the Cherokee Nation west of the Mississippi by the deadline of May 1838. The army rounded up about 16,000 people into camps and marched them to Indian Territory; around 4,000 died of disease, cold and hunger. The removal completed the dispossession of the Southeast's nations and became the defining case of a treaty right overridden by settler demand.",
 now="The Cherokee rebuilt a government in Oklahoma and are today one of the largest Native nations in the United States; the removal is the reference case in every dispute over treaty rights and federal obligations to tribes.",
 story="The Supreme Court had ruled in 1832 that Georgia's laws had no force in Cherokee territory, and President Andrew Jackson declined to enforce it. The Treaty of New Echota of 1835, signed by a minority faction, set May 1838 as the deadline for removal. In May 1838 General Winfield Scott's troops rounded up about 16,000 Cherokee into stockades. Most were marched west in detachments through the autumn and winter of 1838-39 to Indian Territory, now Oklahoma.",
 why="Georgia had already distributed Cherokee land to white settlers by lottery in 1832. The treaty gave the army a legal basis, and Principal Chief John Ross's petitions to Congress failed.",
 then="Around 4,000 Cherokee died in the camps and on the march. The nation re-established its government at Tahlequah in 1839, and the signers of the New Echota treaty, including Major Ridge and Elias Boudinot, were killed by other Cherokee that June.",
 fact="The Cherokee had won their case at the Supreme Court in 1832; Jackson ignored the ruling."),
"na184537": dict(
 bluf="Southern politicians and expansionists wanted Texas in the Union as a slave state, and President James K. Polk had won the 1844 election on annexation. Congress admitted Texas by joint resolution in 1845 after the Senate had refused a treaty the year before. Mexico, which still claimed Texas, broke off relations, the border dispute became war within a year, and 'Manifest Destiny' gave expansion a creed that ran to 1898.",
 now="'Manifest Destiny' gave American expansion a moral claim that shaped policy from the Pacific coast to the overseas empire of 1898.",
 story="Texas had broken from Mexico in 1836 and sought admission to the United States as a slave state. The Senate rejected an annexation treaty in 1844. Outgoing President John Tyler signed a joint resolution of annexation on 1 March 1845, and President James K. Polk, who had won the election on expansion, signed Texas into the Union on 29 December 1845. That summer New York journalist John O'Sullivan coined 'Manifest Destiny' for the country's claimed right to spread across the continent.",
 why="Southern politicians wanted another slave state in the Senate. Expansionists wanted the continent and feared Britain would make Texas a client. Texas wanted protection from Mexico and relief from its debts.",
 then="Mexico broke off diplomatic relations in March 1845. The dispute over whether Texas ended at the Nueces or the Rio Grande gave Polk his occasion for war in 1846, and the Wilmot Proviso of 1846 reopened the fight over slavery in any land taken.",
 fact="Texas entered the Union with the right, written into the resolution, to divide itself into as many as five states."),
"na184638": dict(
 bluf="US President James K. Polk wanted California and its Pacific ports and Mexico refused to sell; he sent troops into the disputed strip north of the Rio Grande and got his war. US forces took California and New Mexico and captured Mexico City in September 1847. Mexico gave up about half its territory, the United States became a continental power with a Pacific coast, and the fight over slavery in the new lands split its parties along sectional lines.",
 now="The war took half of Mexico's territory and gave the United States its Pacific coast, which settled its continental shape and reopened the slavery question over the new lands; Mexican politics still carries the memory.",
 story="Mexico had never accepted Texas's independence or its annexation, and the two countries disputed whether the border ran along the Nueces or the Rio Grande. US President James K. Polk sent General Zachary Taylor's army into the disputed zone, and after a clash in April 1846 he told Congress that Mexico had 'shed American blood upon American soil'. Congress declared war on 13 May 1846. US forces took California and New Mexico, and General Winfield Scott, after landing at Veracruz in March 1847, captured Mexico City on 14 September 1847.",
 why="Polk wanted California and its ports; Mexico's governments, unstable and in debt, could neither sell nor defend it. 'Manifest Destiny' supplied the public case.",
 trig="A clash between US and Mexican troops in the disputed land north of the Rio Grande in April 1846.",
 then="Mexico ceded about half its territory, including California and New Mexico, in the Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo of February 1848 for $15 million. The Wilmot Proviso's attempt to bar slavery from the new lands split both parties along sectional lines. Many Civil War generals, including Ulysses S. Grant and Robert E. Lee, first fought here.",
 fact="Grant, who served in it as a lieutenant, later called it one of the most unjust wars ever waged by a stronger against a weaker nation."),
"na184839": dict(
 bluf="Gold was found at Sutter's Mill in January 1848, nine days before Mexico ceded California to the United States. About 300,000 people arrived from the eastern states, Latin America, Europe, Australia and China, and California became a state within two years. The rush tied the Pacific coast to the national economy and brought killings, disease and starvation that cut the Native population from about 150,000 to about 30,000 by 1870.",
 now="The rush made California a state in 1850 without passing through territorial status, which forced the Compromise of 1850, and it set the Pacific coast's pattern of immigration from Asia and Latin America that shapes its politics still.",
 story="In January 1848, days before Mexico signed California over to the United States, carpenter James Marshall found gold flakes in the tailrace of a sawmill he was building for Swiss settler John Sutter on the American River. Word spread slowly until US President James K. Polk confirmed the find in his message to Congress in December 1848. About 300,000 people then poured in from the eastern United States, Latin America, Europe, Australia and China. San Francisco grew from about 1,000 people to some 25,000 within two years.",
 why="Gold lay in placer deposits a man with a pan could work, and California had almost no government to control access. Steamships and the press carried the word around the world within months.",
 then="California entered the Union as a free state in 1850. The easy gold was gone by the mid-1850s and mining passed to companies. The Native population fell from about 150,000 to about 30,000 by 1870 through killings, disease and starvation, which California's governor formally acknowledged as genocide in 2019.",
 fact="San Francisco grew from about 1,000 people to around 25,000 in two years. Levi Strauss arrived in 1853 to sell dry goods to miners; the riveted jeans came twenty years later."),
"na184840": dict(
 bluf="Abolitionists Elizabeth Cady Stanton and Lucretia Mott, refused seats at an anti-slavery convention in London in 1840 because they were women, called a convention to claim women's rights for themselves. About 300 people met at Seneca Falls in July 1848, and 100 signed a Declaration of Sentiments that demanded equal rights, including the vote. It launched the organised women's rights movement in the United States and set its agenda for the 72 years to the 19th Amendment.",
 now="It launched the organised women's rights movement in the United States; its demands, from the vote to property rights, set the agenda until the 19th Amendment in 1920.",
 story="Elizabeth Cady Stanton and Lucretia Mott had been refused seats as delegates at the World Anti-Slavery Convention in London in 1840. In July 1848 they called a two-day convention at the Wesleyan Chapel in Seneca Falls, New York. Stanton's Declaration of Sentiments, modelled on the Declaration of Independence, held that 'all men and women are created equal' and listed women's grievances. The resolution demanding the vote was the only one to pass without unanimity; abolitionist Frederick Douglass spoke for it.",
 why="Women abolitionists had learned to organise and speak in the anti-slavery movement and were shut out of its leadership. Married women in most states could own no property and had no legal standing apart from their husbands.",
 then="A national women's rights convention met at Worcester in 1850 and most years after. The movement split in 1869 over the 15th Amendment, which enfranchised Black men and left women out; the two wings reunited in 1890. Women won the national vote in 1920.",
 fact="Of the 100 signers, only one, Charlotte Woodward, lived to see women win the vote in 1920."),
"na185041": dict(
 bluf="Kentucky Senator Henry Clay set out to hold the Union together by settling every dispute raised by the land taken from Mexico in one package. Illinois Senator Stephen Douglas pushed it through in pieces: California entered free, the rest of the Mexican cession was left to its settlers, and the South got a Fugitive Slave Act that made Northerners help return people who had escaped. The compromise bought ten years, and the Fugitive Slave Act turned Northern opinion against slavery faster than any abolitionist had.",
 now="The compromise bought a decade and used up the material for another; the Fugitive Slave Act put slavery in Northern streets and courtrooms, which turned moderate opinion against it between 1850 and 1860.",
 story="California applied to enter as a free state in 1849, which would have given free states a Senate majority, while Texas claimed half of New Mexico and the South demanded a stronger fugitive slave law. Kentucky Senator Henry Clay proposed an omnibus settlement in January 1850 and Massachusetts Senator Daniel Webster backed it. The omnibus failed in July; Illinois Senator Stephen Douglas passed its parts as separate bills in September. California entered free, New Mexico and Utah were organised with no ban on slavery, Texas gave up its claim for $10 million, the slave trade ended in Washington, and a Fugitive Slave Act put federal commissioners at the service of slaveholders.",
 why="The Mexican cession reopened the question Missouri had settled. California's admission threatened the balance of 15 slave and 15 free states, and Southern leaders had met at Nashville in June 1850 to discuss secession.",
 then="Federal marshals seized people who had escaped in Boston, Syracuse and other Northern cities, and crowds fought to free them. Harriet Beecher Stowe's 'Uncle Tom's Cabin' of 1852 sold 300,000 copies in its first year. The Kansas-Nebraska Act of 1854 broke the settlement.",
 fact="President Zachary Taylor, who opposed the compromise, died in July 1850; his successor Millard Fillmore signed every bill."),
"na185442": dict(
 bluf="Illinois Senator Stephen Douglas wanted the Nebraska territory organised for a transcontinental railroad through Chicago and needed Southern votes to do it. He gave them the repeal of the Missouri Compromise line and let settlers vote on slavery. Pro- and anti-slavery settlers fought over Kansas, the Whig Party broke up, the Republican Party was founded to resist slavery's spread, and the sectional politics that produced secession in 1860 took shape within two years.",
 now="The act replaced national parties with sectional ones: it destroyed the Whigs, created the Republican Party and put the first armed fighting over slavery in Kansas, which made a sectional presidential election in 1860 the likely outcome.",
 story="Illinois Senator Stephen Douglas introduced a bill in January 1854 to organise the Kansas and Nebraska territories, with slavery to be decided by their settlers under 'popular sovereignty'. The bill repealed the Missouri Compromise ban on slavery north of 36°30', and President Franklin Pierce signed it on 30 May 1854. Pro-slavery Missourians and anti-slavery Northerners poured into Kansas; by 1856 the territory had two rival legislatures and open fighting, 'Bleeding Kansas'.",
 why="Douglas wanted a northern railroad route and the organised territory it required. Southern senators would vote for it only with the Missouri ban gone.",
 then="Anti-slavery Whigs, Democrats and Free Soilers formed the Republican Party in 1854; it carried most of the North in 1856 and elected Abraham Lincoln in 1860. About 55 people died in Kansas by 1859, and the territory entered as a free state in January 1861.",
 fact="Abolitionist John Brown and his sons killed five pro-slavery settlers at Pottawatomie Creek in May 1856, days after a South Carolina congressman had beaten Massachusetts Senator Charles Sumner unconscious on the Senate floor."),
"na185743": dict(
 bluf="Chief Justice Roger Taney and the Supreme Court's Southern majority set out to settle the slavery question by law, with President-elect James Buchanan's encouragement. They ruled that no Black American could be a citizen and that Congress lacked the power to bar slavery from any territory. The ruling declared the Republican Party's whole platform unconstitutional, which made compromise impossible and drove the North to the Republicans in 1860.",
 now="The ruling is the standard example of a court entrenching injustice; it was reversed by the 14th Amendment of 1868, whose citizenship clause was written against it.",
 story="Dred Scott, an enslaved man, sued for freedom in 1846 on the ground that his owner had taken him to live in the free state of Illinois and the free Wisconsin Territory. On 6 March 1857 Chief Justice Roger Taney ruled for a 7-2 majority that no Black person, free or enslaved, could be a citizen with standing to sue, and that the Missouri Compromise ban on slavery in the territories had been unconstitutional. President James Buchanan, inaugurated two days earlier, had lobbied a Northern justice to join the majority.",
 why="Buchanan and the Court's Southern majority hoped a sweeping ruling would take slavery out of politics. Taney held that the founders had meant no Black person to be part of the political community.",
 then="Republicans made the ruling their campaign issue; Abraham Lincoln argued in 1858 that it was one step from making slavery lawful in every state. Northern Democrats split from Southern over whether settlers could still bar slavery, and the divided party lost the 1860 election. The 14th Amendment of 1868 overturned the ruling.",
 fact="Scott was freed by his owners within three months of losing and died of tuberculosis in 1858."),
"na186144": dict(
 bluf="Eleven Southern states seceded to protect slavery after Abraham Lincoln won the presidency on a pledge to stop its spread; Lincoln fought to hold the Union together. Four years of war killed 620,000 to 750,000 soldiers and ended with the Confederacy destroyed and slavery abolished. The Union's victory fixed the supremacy of the national government, and the collapse of Reconstruction left the question of Black citizenship unsettled for another century.",
 now="The war ended slavery, settled that the Union could not be left, and created a federal state with an income tax, a national currency and a standing claim on its citizens that the republic of 1789 lacked.",
 story="Republican Abraham Lincoln won the election of November 1860 without carrying a single Southern state. South Carolina seceded in December and six more states followed by February 1861, forming the Confederate States with Mississippi Senator Jefferson Davis as president. On 12 April 1861 Confederate batteries fired on Fort Sumter in Charleston harbour, and the garrison surrendered the next day. Lincoln called for 75,000 volunteers, and four more states, Virginia among them, joined the Confederacy.",
 why="Decades of conflict over slavery's expansion came first. The South's economy rested on the labour of about 4 million enslaved people, and the free-labour North had the population and the votes to outgrow it.",
 trig="Lincoln's election in November 1860 and the Confederate attack on Fort Sumter in April 1861.",
 then="Some 620,000 to 750,000 soldiers died. The Union's advantages in industry and manpower, emancipation and the enlistment of about 180,000 Black soldiers brought victory in April 1865. The 13th, 14th and 15th Amendments ended slavery, defined citizenship and protected the vote, and Southern states nullified much of the last two after 1877.",
 fact="No one was killed in the 34-hour bombardment of Fort Sumter; the war's first death came when a gun exploded during the surrender salute."),
"na186345": dict(
 bluf="US President Abraham Lincoln wanted to strip the Confederacy of its labour, recruit Black soldiers and make it impossible for Britain or France to recognise the South. On 1 January 1863 he declared enslaved people in the rebel states 'forever free' under his war powers. The Union's war became a war against slavery, about 180,000 Black men joined its army, and emancipation was irreversible before the 13th Amendment made it law in 1865.",
 now="The proclamation turned the war into a war against slavery, kept Britain and France out of it, and brought 180,000 Black soldiers into the Union army, which made emancipation irreversible before the amendment of 1865.",
 story="Enslaved people had been fleeing to Union lines since 1861, and Congress had declared them confiscated property. US President Abraham Lincoln issued a preliminary proclamation on 22 September 1862, five days after the Union stopped Lee at Antietam. On 1 January 1863 the final proclamation declared enslaved people in areas still in rebellion 'forever free' and opened the army and navy to Black men. It left the loyal slave states and Union-held districts untouched, so it freed few people at once; every Union advance thereafter brought freedom.",
 why="Lincoln wanted to weaken the Confederacy's labour force, recruit Black soldiers and make it politically impossible for Britain and France to back the South. Enslaved people fleeing to Union lines had already made slavery a war issue.",
 then="About 180,000 Black soldiers fought for the Union, Britain and France stayed out, and the 13th Amendment abolished slavery nationwide in December 1865.",
 fact="Juneteenth, a federal holiday since 2021, marks the day the news reached the last enslaved Texans."),
"na186346": dict(
 bluf="Confederate General Robert E. Lee invaded Pennsylvania to win a battle on Northern soil that would break Northern will and bring peace. Union General George Meade's army held the high ground for three days and broke Pickett's Charge on the third; about 50,000 men on both sides were killed, wounded or captured. Lee never invaded again, Vicksburg fell the next day, and the Confederacy's chance of winning by arms was gone.",
 now="With Vicksburg's fall the next day, Gettysburg ended the Confederacy's capacity for offensive war; Lincoln's address there in November 1863 redefined the Union's purpose as equality and government by the people, the terms in which American democracy has described itself since.",
 story="After his victory at Chancellorsville in May 1863, Confederate General Robert E. Lee led about 75,000 men into Pennsylvania. The armies met at Gettysburg on 1 July 1863, and Union General George Meade, in command for three days, held the ridges south of the town. On 3 July Lee sent about 12,500 men against the Union centre in Pickett's Charge; half of them fell. About 50,000 men were killed, wounded or captured over the three days.",
 why="Lee wanted to feed his army off Northern farms, draw Union forces out of Virginia and win a victory that would strengthen the Northern peace movement. Chancellorsville had convinced him his army could beat any force it met.",
 then="Lee retreated to Virginia on 4 July, the day Vicksburg surrendered to Ulysses S. Grant and gave the Union the Mississippi. The South never invaded the North again. Lincoln dedicated the cemetery on 19 November 1863 with a 270-word address.",
 fact="Lincoln's address ran about 270 words and took two minutes; orator Edward Everett, the main speaker before him, talked for two hours."),
"na186547": dict(
 bluf="Union General Ulysses S. Grant set out to destroy the Army of Northern Virginia, and Confederate General Robert E. Lee tried to break away to join the Confederacy's last army in North Carolina. Grant's cavalry cut off Lee's starving column at Appomattox and Lee surrendered on 9 April 1865. The surrender ended the war in all but name, and Grant's terms, which sent the men home with their horses and no prosecutions, set the pattern of a peace built on reunion ahead of justice for the freed people.",
 now="Appomattox fixed the terms of reunion: no treason trials, officers paroled, the Confederate army dissolved into the civilian population; the leniency made reconciliation between white North and South possible and left the former Confederates in place to resist Reconstruction.",
 story="After a ten-month siege at Petersburg, Confederate General Robert E. Lee abandoned the city and Richmond on 2 April 1865 and marched west, hoping to turn south to join General Joseph Johnston's army in North Carolina. Union cavalry under General Philip Sheridan got ahead of him at Appomattox Court House. On 9 April 1865 Lee surrendered his roughly 28,000 men to Union General Ulysses S. Grant in the parlour of Wilmer McLean's house. Grant paroled the men and let them keep their horses.",
 why="The Confederacy was outnumbered and outproduced; Sherman's march had wrecked its heartland, and the Petersburg siege had stretched Lee's army to breaking point. Desertion emptied his ranks over the final winter.",
 then="Johnston surrendered to Sherman on 26 April, and the last Confederate forces in the field gave up in June. Lincoln was shot on 14 April, five days after Appomattox. The war's dead are estimated at 620,000 to 750,000 soldiers, the most of any American conflict.",
 fact="The surrender was signed in the parlour of Wilmer McLean, who had moved to Appomattox from Manassas after the war's first battle was fought across his farm."),
"na186547b": dict(
 bluf="Actor and Confederate sympathiser John Wilkes Booth planned to kill the president, vice president and secretary of state in one night to throw the Union government into chaos and revive the Confederate cause. He shot Abraham Lincoln at Ford's Theatre on 14 April 1865; Lincoln died the next morning, Secretary of State William Seward survived a stabbing, and the attack on the vice president never came. Andrew Johnson, a Tennessee Democrat hostile to Black rights, took charge of Reconstruction and tilted the peace toward the defeated South.",
 now="Lincoln's death put Reconstruction in the hands of a president who pardoned Confederates wholesale and vetoed civil rights bills, which produced the clash between Congress and the executive of 1866-68 and weakened protection of the freed people from the start.",
 story="On the night of 14 April 1865, five days after Lee's surrender, actor John Wilkes Booth entered the presidential box at Ford's Theatre in Washington and shot Abraham Lincoln in the back of the head. Lincoln died at 7.22 the next morning in a boarding house across the street. The same night Booth's accomplice Lewis Powell stabbed Secretary of State William Seward, who survived; the man assigned to kill Vice President Andrew Johnson lost his nerve. Booth was shot dead in a Virginia barn on 26 April, and four accomplices were hanged in July.",
 why="Booth believed killing the Union's leaders would throw its government into chaos and give the South a last chance. Lincoln's speech of 11 April endorsing limited Black suffrage had enraged him.",
 then="Andrew Johnson became president, pardoned most former Confederates and let Southern states pass Black Codes restricting the freed people. Congress overrode his vetoes, passed the 14th Amendment and impeached him in 1868; he survived removal by one vote.",
 fact="On the day he was shot, Lincoln signed the law creating the Secret Service, originally to fight counterfeit money."),
"na186547c": dict(
 bluf="US President Abraham Lincoln and the Republicans wanted abolition written into the Constitution so that no court or peace settlement could undo the Emancipation Proclamation. The Senate passed the amendment in April 1864 and the House, after Lincoln's lobbying, in January 1865; ratification came in December. Slavery ended by law for about four million people, and the exception for punishment of crime was used after Reconstruction to lease convicts as forced labour across the South.",
 now="The exception for punishment of crime was the legal basis of convict leasing from the 1870s to the 1920s and is the subject of today's argument over prison labour.",
 story="The Emancipation Proclamation was a war measure that covered only rebel areas. The Senate passed an abolition amendment in April 1864; the House fell short of two thirds in June. After his re-election US President Abraham Lincoln pressed wavering Democrats, and the House passed it on 31 January 1865 by 119 votes to 56. Georgia's ratification on 6 December 1865 made it law: slavery and involuntary servitude were abolished 'except as a punishment for crime whereof the party shall have been duly convicted'. About four million people were freed.",
 why="The proclamation rested on war powers that would lapse with peace, and the courts might have ruled against it. Lincoln wanted abolition settled before the Southern states returned to Congress.",
 then="The 14th Amendment of 1868 defined citizenship and equal protection, and the 15th of 1870 barred denial of the vote by race. Southern states leased convicts to mines, railways and plantations from the 1870s, and most of the convicts were Black men convicted under vagrancy laws.",
 fact="Mississippi's legislature rejected the amendment in 1865 and ratified it only in 1995; the paperwork reached the federal register in 2013."),
"na186748": dict(
 bluf="Russian Tsar Alexander II wanted cash after the Crimean War and feared losing an indefensible Alaska to Britain; US Secretary of State William Seward wanted the territory and the Pacific position. The United States paid $7.2 million, about 2 cents an acre, in 1867. Russia left North America, and a purchase mocked as 'Seward's Folly' yielded gold from the 1890s, oil from the 1960s and a Cold War frontier facing the Soviet Union.",
 now="Alaska gives the United States an Arctic coastline and a Pacific position 55 miles from Russia, which made it the front line of air defence in the Cold War and makes it the country's stake in Arctic shipping and resources now.",
 story="Russia had held Alaska since the 1700s through a fur company whose profits were falling. After the Crimean War of 1853-56 Russian Tsar Alexander II concluded the colony could never be defended against Britain and offered it to the United States. US Secretary of State William Seward and Russian minister Eduard de Stoeckl signed the treaty on 30 March 1867 for $7.2 million, about 2 cents an acre. The Senate ratified it in April and the transfer took place at Sitka in October.",
 why="Russia needed cash after the Crimean War, could not defend Alaska, and preferred selling it to the United States to seeing Britain take it.",
 then="Gold strikes in the Yukon and at Nome in the 1890s brought the first rush of settlers. Alaska became a state in 1959, oil was found at Prudhoe Bay in 1968, and its bases faced the Soviet Union across the Bering Strait through the Cold War.",
 fact="Critics mocked the purchase as 'Seward's Folly' and 'Andrew Johnson's polar bear garden'. It cost about 2 cents an acre."),
"na186949": dict(
 bluf="Congress wanted California bound to the Union and the West opened to settlement, and it paid two companies in land grants and loans to build the line. The Central Pacific, with thousands of Chinese workers, and the Union Pacific, with Irish labourers and Civil War veterans, met at Promontory Summit, Utah, on 10 May 1869. A crossing of months fell to about a week, a national market followed, and the Plains nations and the bison herds they lived on were destroyed within two decades.",
 now="Its mix of public subsidy and private profit set the pattern for American infrastructure, and the time zones adopted for the railways in 1883 still govern daily life.",
 story="The Pacific Railway Act of 1862, passed with the South out of Congress, chartered the Union Pacific to build west from Omaha and the Central Pacific to build east from Sacramento, with government loans and alternate sections of public land along the route. The Central Pacific hired thousands of Chinese workers, who cut the line through the Sierra Nevada; the Union Pacific hired Irish immigrants and Civil War veterans. The two met at Promontory Summit, Utah, on 10 May 1869, where a golden spike was driven.",
 why="Congress wanted to bind California to the Union and open the West to settlement and markets, and offered railroad companies land grants and loans to do it. Private capital alone would have left the empty interior unbuilt.",
 then="Crossing the country fell from months to about a week. Railways brought settlers and hunters to the Plains; the bison herds were shot out by the mid-1880s and the Plains nations confined to reservations. The Crédit Mobilier scandal of 1872 exposed Union Pacific insiders who had paid congressmen in shares.",
 fact="Railroads were the reason the United States adopted standard time zones in 1883; before that every town kept its own time."),
"na187650": dict(
 bluf="The United States wanted the Black Hills after gold was found there in 1874 and ordered every Lakota onto a reservation by January 1876; Lakota chief Sitting Bull and war leader Crazy Horse gathered the bands that refused. On 25 June 1876 their warriors destroyed Lieutenant Colonel George Custer's column of about 210 men on the Little Bighorn. The victory brought overwhelming retaliation: within a year Congress had seized the Black Hills, Crazy Horse had surrendered and Sitting Bull had fled to Canada.",
 now="The Lakota have refused payment for the Black Hills to this day; a 1980 Supreme Court ruling awarded compensation that, with interest, now exceeds $1 billion.",
 story="The Treaty of Fort Laramie of 1868 had guaranteed the Black Hills to the Lakota. Gold was found there in 1874 by an expedition under Lieutenant Colonel George Custer, prospectors poured in, and the government ordered all Lakota onto reservations by 31 January 1876. Three army columns went after the bands that stayed out. On 25 June 1876 Custer divided his 7th Cavalry and attacked a camp of several thousand Lakota, Cheyenne and Arapaho on the Little Bighorn in Montana; the warriors, with Crazy Horse among their leaders, killed Custer and about 210 men with him.",
 why="The United States broke the 1868 treaty once gold was found and had no intention of removing the miners. The order to come in by January was issued in midwinter, when travel was impossible, and the bands treated it as a declaration of war.",
 then="Within a year Crazy Horse had surrendered, Sitting Bull had fled to Canada and Congress had taken the Black Hills by an act of February 1877. The Supreme Court ruled in 1980 that the taking was unlawful and awarded compensation that the Lakota have refused.",
 fact="Comanche, a cavalry horse found wounded on the field, was the army's only survivor of Custer's column and lived on as a regimental mascot."),
"na187751": dict(
 bluf="Republicans wanted the presidency after a disputed 1876 election, and Southern Democrats wanted the last federal troops out of their statehouses. The deal gave Rutherford B. Hayes the White House and withdrew the troops from South Carolina and Louisiana in April 1877. Federal protection of Black Southerners ended, white-supremacist governments stripped them of the vote by 1900 and built segregation under law, and the settlement held until the civil rights movement of the 1950s and 60s.",
 now="The civil rights movement of the 1950s and 60s was a fight to undo this, and the balance between federal and state control of voting is still contested.",
 story="Democrat Samuel Tilden won the popular vote in November 1876, and the returns from South Carolina, Louisiana and Florida were disputed. A congressional commission awarded every contested elector to Republican Rutherford B. Hayes by 8 votes to 7. Southern Democrats accepted the result on the understanding that Hayes would withdraw the troops guarding the Republican governments in South Carolina and Louisiana; he did so in April 1877, and those governments fell. Southern Democrats, the 'Redeemers', then held every former Confederate state.",
 why="Northern voters had tired of occupying the South, and the depression after 1873 moved politics to money and labour. White paramilitaries had already overturned Mississippi's Republican government by violence in 1875. The disputed election gave both parties a reason to deal.",
 trig="The Compromise of 1877, which gave Republican Rutherford B. Hayes the presidency in exchange for removing federal troops from the South.",
 then="Southern states used poll taxes, literacy tests and violence to strip Black citizens of the vote; Mississippi's constitution of 1890 set the model. The Supreme Court upheld segregation in Plessy v. Ferguson in 1896. Thousands of Black Americans were lynched over the following decades.",
 fact="'Jim Crow' came from a minstrel-show character of the 1830s played by white performer Thomas Rice in blackface."),
"na188252": dict(
 bluf="West Coast politicians and labour leaders wanted Chinese workers out, blaming them for low wages in a depression, and both parties wanted the votes. Congress barred Chinese labourers from entering for ten years and President Chester A. Arthur signed it in May 1882. For the first time the United States excluded immigrants by nationality, which set the template for the race-based quotas of 1924, and the ban held until 1943.",
 now="It is the root of US laws that decide who may enter by origin; Congress formally expressed regret for it in 2011 and 2012.",
 story="Chinese workers had come to the West Coast since the gold rush and had built the Central Pacific railway. The depression of the 1870s brought anti-Chinese riots and the Workingmen's Party in California under the slogan 'The Chinese must go'. Congress passed a 20-year ban that President Chester A. Arthur vetoed in April 1882; he signed a 10-year ban on 6 May 1882. It barred Chinese labourers from entering and Chinese residents from naturalising.",
 why="Chinese workers on the West Coast were blamed for low wages during the depression of the 1870s, and politicians of both parties courted anti-Chinese voters in a closely divided electorate.",
 then="The Geary Act of 1892 renewed the ban and required Chinese residents to carry papers; the exclusion was made permanent in 1902. The Immigration Act of 1924 barred all Asian immigration and set national quotas. Congress repealed exclusion in 1943, when China was a wartime ally, with a quota of 105 a year.",
 fact="The Supreme Court ruled in 1898 that a man born in San Francisco to Chinese parents was a citizen, which fixed birthright citizenship for everyone born in the country."),
"na188653": dict(
 bluf="Chicago's workers struck for an eight-hour day on 1 May 1886, and anarchists called a rally in Haymarket Square after police killed strikers at the McCormick works. Someone threw a bomb at the police; seven officers and at least four civilians died, eight anarchists were convicted on evidence that tied none of them to the bomb, and four were hanged. In the United States the affair fed fear of immigrant radicals and broke the Knights of Labor; abroad it made 1 May the workers' day.",
 now="May 1 became International Workers' Day in honour of the Haymarket martyrs, almost everywhere except the United States; at home the backlash moved labour from the Knights' broad crusade to the AFL's craft unionism, the pattern until the 1930s.",
 story="About 300,000 workers struck across the country on 1 May 1886 for the eight-hour day. On 3 May Chicago police fired on strikers at the McCormick reaper works and killed at least two. Anarchist leaders called a protest at Haymarket Square on 4 May; as police moved to disperse it, someone threw a bomb that killed one officer at once and six more from wounds, and the police fired into the crowd. Eight anarchists were tried for murder; the bomber was never identified. Four were hanged on 11 November 1887, one killed himself in his cell, and three were pardoned by Illinois Governor John Peter Altgeld in 1893.",
 why="Workers were fighting for an eight-hour day amid violent clashes with police and employers. Chicago's anarchist press had called for arms, which the prosecution used in place of evidence.",
 then="Fear of immigrant radicals spread, employers broke the eight-hour strikes, and the Knights of Labor fell from about 700,000 members in 1886 to a remnant within a decade. The American Federation of Labor, founded that December, took its place. The Second International named 1 May the workers' day in 1889.",
 fact="The police statue erected in Haymarket Square was blown up twice, in 1969 and 1970, by the Weathermen; the martyrs' monument in a Chicago cemetery draws unions from around the world."),
"na188653b": dict(
 bluf="Craft union leaders, with cigar maker Samuel Gompers at their head, wanted a national federation that would bargain for wages and hours and leave politics and revolution alone. They founded the American Federation of Labor at Columbus, Ohio, in December 1886, weeks after Haymarket. Its 'business unionism' became the pattern of American labour, which never built a mass socialist party on the European model.",
 now="The AFL's focus on wages and conditions rather than socialism shaped American unions, which remain more pragmatic and less tied to a political party than many in Europe.",
 story="The Knights of Labor, which organised skilled and unskilled workers together, was collapsing after Haymarket. Delegates from craft unions met at Columbus, Ohio, on 8 December 1886 and elected cigar maker Samuel Gompers president of the new American Federation of Labor. Gompers set its course on 'bread-and-butter' goals, wages, hours and conditions, won by strikes and contracts with employers, and kept it from endorsing any party.",
 why="After the Haymarket backlash weakened the more radical Knights of Labor, craft unions wanted a stronger national organisation of their own. Skilled workers had bargaining power the unskilled lacked and wanted to use it without carrying them.",
 then="The AFL reached about 2 million members by 1914 and more than 4 million by 1920. Gompers led it until his death in 1924. It merged with the industrial unions of the CIO in 1955.",
 fact="Gompers, asked what labour wanted, answered 'More'."),
"na189054": dict(
 bluf="The army set out to disarm a band of about 350 Lakota under Chief Spotted Elk whose Ghost Dance observance it feared, two weeks after Sitting Bull was killed in an arrest attempt. A rifle went off during the search for weapons on 29 December 1890, and the 7th Cavalry's rifles and Hotchkiss guns killed about 250 people, many of them women and children. Armed Native resistance in the United States ended there, and decades of allotment, boarding schools and forced assimilation followed unopposed.",
 now="Wounded Knee closed the Plains wars and opened the assimilation era; the American Indian Movement occupied the site for 71 days in 1973 to protest broken treaties, and the massacre remains the reference point in debates over Native sovereignty and land.",
 story="The Ghost Dance, a religious movement promising the return of the bison and the dead, spread through the Lakota reservations in 1890, and the army moved in. Lakota chief Sitting Bull was killed by reservation police on 15 December during an attempt to arrest him. Chief Spotted Elk led his band toward the Pine Ridge agency and surrendered to the 7th Cavalry, which surrounded the camp at Wounded Knee Creek with four Hotchkiss guns. On 29 December 1890, as soldiers searched for weapons, a rifle went off and the troops opened fire; about 250 Lakota and 25 soldiers died. The same year the Census Bureau declared the frontier closed.",
 why="The army feared the Ghost Dance, a movement promising the return of the old world, and reservation agents had called for troops. The killing of Sitting Bull days earlier had sent Lakota bands fleeing across the reservations.",
 then="The Dawes Act of 1887 was pushed through the reservations, breaking tribal land into private plots and selling the 'surplus' to settlers; Native landholding fell by about two thirds by 1934. Children were sent to boarding schools to strip them of language and culture.",
 fact="The army awarded 20 Medals of Honor for Wounded Knee, which Lakota descendants have campaigned to have rescinded."),
"na189655": dict(
 bluf="A committee of New Orleans Creoles of colour set out to test Louisiana's railway segregation law by having shoemaker Homer Plessy, who was one-eighth Black, arrested in a whites-only car. The Supreme Court ruled 7-1 in 1896 that separate facilities were lawful if 'equal'. Segregation gained constitutional approval, spread through every public institution in the South and stood for 58 years, until Brown v. Board of Education in 1954.",
 now="'Separate but equal' governed American law for 58 years until Brown v. Board overturned it in 1954, a reminder that courts can entrench injustice as well as end it.",
 story="Louisiana's Separate Car Act of 1890 required railways to seat Black and white passengers apart. The Comité des Citoyens of New Orleans arranged for shoemaker Homer Plessy, one-eighth Black and able to pass as white, to buy a first-class ticket and sit in a whites-only car on 7 June 1892; he was arrested by prior arrangement. On 18 May 1896 the Supreme Court ruled 7-1 that the law was constitutional under the 14th Amendment provided facilities were equal. Justice John Marshall Harlan dissented alone: 'Our Constitution is colour-blind'.",
 why="Southern states were rolling back the rights of Reconstruction, and the committee wanted a federal ruling before segregation hardened. The Court's majority read the 14th Amendment as guaranteeing legal rather than social equality.",
 then="Segregation spread by law through schools, trains, restaurants and hospitals across the South, and the 'equal' half of the formula was ignored. Southern states stripped Black citizens of the vote in the same decade. Brown v. Board of Education overturned the doctrine for schools in 1954.",
 fact="Plessy pleaded guilty in 1897 and paid a $25 fine; Louisiana pardoned him posthumously in 2022."),
"na190157": dict(
 bluf="Theodore Roosevelt became president at 42 when William McKinley died of an assassin's bullet in September 1901, and he set out to make the federal government the referee between big business and the public. He broke up the Northern Securities railway trust, regulated food and medicine, settled a coal strike and put about 230 million acres under federal protection. Washington's role in the economy grew, and the Progressive Era that followed brought the income tax, direct election of senators and women's suffrage.",
 now="Antitrust law, food safety rules and national parks are his lasting legacy, and debates over breaking up today's tech giants revive his arguments.",
 story="Anarchist Leon Czolgosz shot President William McKinley at the Pan-American Exposition in Buffalo on 6 September 1901; McKinley died eight days later and Vice President Theodore Roosevelt was sworn in. Roosevelt sued to break up the Northern Securities railway combine in 1902 and won in 1904, mediated the anthracite coal strike of 1902, and signed the Pure Food and Drug Act and the Meat Inspection Act in 1906. He created five national parks, 18 national monuments and some 150 national forests.",
 why="President McKinley was shot by an anarchist in Buffalo in September 1901 and died eight days later. Roosevelt believed that unregulated corporate power would bring socialism unless government curbed it.",
 then="The Progressive Era brought the income tax in 1913, direct election of senators in 1913 and women's suffrage in 1920. Roosevelt won the 1904 election outright, declined to run in 1908 and split the Republicans in 1912.",
 fact="Party boss Mark Hanna had put Roosevelt in the vice presidency to get him out of New York politics, and on McKinley's death is reported to have said that 'that damned cowboy' was now president."),
"na190358": dict(
 bluf="Bicycle makers Orville and Wilbur Wright of Dayton, Ohio, set out to solve the problem that had killed the glider pioneers: control. They built a powered machine with wing-warping and a movable rudder, and on 17 December 1903 at Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, it flew 120 feet in 12 seconds. Powered flight became an engineering fact; within 15 years aircraft were fighting over the Western Front, and within fifty, air travel and air power had shrunk every distance.",
 now="Aviation shrank every distance within half a century, made air power the decisive arm of war from 1940 and now carries about 4 billion passenger trips a year, with emissions that are part of the climate argument.",
 story="Orville and Wilbur Wright, bicycle makers from Dayton, Ohio, had tested gliders at Kitty Hawk since 1900 and built their own wind tunnel to correct the published lift tables. Their 1903 Flyer had a 12-horsepower engine they built themselves and controls for pitch, roll and yaw. On 17 December 1903 Orville flew it 120 feet in 12 seconds; the fourth flight that day, with Wilbur, covered 852 feet in 59 seconds.",
 why="Earlier builders had chased lift and power; the Wrights treated control as the problem and solved it with wing-warping and a movable rudder. Lighter petrol engines and decades of glider data made the rest possible.",
 then="The Wrights flew for the US Army in 1908 and sold it a plane in 1909. Within 15 years planes were fighting in the First World War. Air power reshaped war, from strategic bombing to aircraft carriers, and jet airliners from the 1950s made mass travel possible.",
 fact="The first flight lasted 12 seconds and covered 120 feet, less than the wingspan of a Boeing 747."),
"na190859": dict(
 bluf="Henry Ford wanted a car that a working family could afford, built from standard parts by unskilled labour. The Model T of 1908 and the moving assembly line of 1913 cut build time from about 12 hours to about 90 minutes and the price from $850 to about $260. Mass production and mass consumption became the model of industry, and the car reshaped cities, suburbs and the politics of oil.",
 now="Fordism, high-volume production with wages high enough to buy the output, became the model of industrial economies from the 1920s to the 1970s; mass car ownership built the suburbs and highways and made oil a matter of national security.",
 story="Ford Motor Company put the Model T on sale in October 1908 at $850, a simple car built from interchangeable parts. In 1913 its Highland Park plant in Detroit introduced the moving assembly line, which cut the time to build a chassis from about 12 hours to about 90 minutes. In January 1914 Henry Ford doubled his workers' pay to $5 a day, which cut turnover and let them afford the car. The price fell to about $260 by the mid-1920s.",
 why="Ford wanted a car ordinary people could afford, and standardised parts and the moving assembly line made it possible. Rising wages and cheap steel and petrol gave him the market.",
 then="By 1927 Ford had sold 15 million Model Ts. Mass car ownership created suburbs, highways and dependence on oil. General Motors overtook Ford in the late 1920s by offering styles and annual models.",
 fact="Ford said customers could have it 'any colour, as long as it's black'. Black was chosen because it dried fastest on the line."),
"na191360": dict(
 bluf="Progressive reformers in both parties wanted federal revenue that fell on the wealthy rather than on tariffs that raised everyone's prices, after the Supreme Court had struck down an income tax in 1895. Congress proposed the amendment in 1909 and the states ratified it in February 1913. The federal government gained a revenue source that could grow without limit, which financed two world wars, the New Deal and the modern American state.",
 now="The individual income tax is still the largest single source of federal revenue, which is why fights over its rates are the central fight of American fiscal politics.",
 story="The Supreme Court had struck down an income tax in 1895 as a direct tax that had to be apportioned among the states by population. Congress passed the amendment in July 1909 under President William Howard Taft, and Delaware's ratification on 3 February 1913 completed it. The Revenue Act of October 1913 set rates of 1 to 7 per cent on incomes above $3,000, which exempted all but about 1 per cent of households.",
 why="Progressive reformers wanted revenue that fell on the wealthy rather than tariffs that raised prices for everyone. Conservatives in Congress proposed the amendment expecting the states to reject it.",
 then="Rates rose to 77 per cent on the highest incomes to pay for the First World War. The tax paid for the New Deal and the Second World War, when withholding from wages made most workers taxpayers, and became the government's main source of revenue.",
 fact="The first Form 1040 ran to four pages, including the instructions."),
"na191360b": dict(
 bluf="President Woodrow Wilson and Congress wanted a lender of last resort after the Panic of 1907 had been stopped only by banker J. P. Morgan's private rescue. The Federal Reserve Act of December 1913 created twelve regional reserve banks under a board in Washington. The United States gained a central bank; its failure to stop bank collapses after 1929 deepened the Depression, and its later authority over interest rates made it the most powerful economic institution in the world.",
 now="The Fed sets interest rates that move markets worldwide, and its independence from political pressure is a recurring battle, intensified in 2025.",
 story="Bank panics in 1873, 1893 and 1907 had closed banks and businesses across the country, and in 1907 banker J. P. Morgan locked the leading financiers in his library until they agreed to a rescue. A National Monetary Commission under Senator Nelson Aldrich proposed a central bank; Democrats rejected its banker-run design. President Woodrow Wilson signed the Federal Reserve Act on 23 December 1913: twelve regional reserve banks owned by member banks, overseen by a board appointed by the president, with power to issue currency and lend to banks.",
 why="Repeated banking panics, ending in 1907, and the lack of any lender of last resort, which left the system depending on private bankers. Wilson's Democrats wanted public control, the banks wanted private, and the act split the difference.",
 then="The Fed's failure to stop bank collapses after 1929 deepened the Great Depression. The Banking Act of 1935 centralised power in the Washington board, and the 1951 accord with the Treasury established its independence in setting rates.",
 fact="The plan was drafted in secret in November 1910 at a club on Jekyll Island, Georgia, by bankers who travelled there using first names only."),
"d-pancho": dict(
 bluf="Mexican revolutionary general Pancho Villa wanted to punish the United States for recognising his rival Venustiano Carranza and to provoke a crisis that would discredit Carranza's government. His men attacked Columbus, New Mexico, on 9 March 1916 and killed 18 Americans. President Woodrow Wilson sent General John J. Pershing and more than 10,000 troops into Mexico for eleven months without catching him, which brought the two countries close to war as Germany was courting Mexico.",
 now="The expedition fixed US military intervention in Mexico as a standing grievance in Mexican politics, and Villa remains a folk hero there, a symbol of defiance toward US power.",
 story="The United States had recognised Venustiano Carranza's government in October 1915 and let his troops cross US territory to defeat Villa at Agua Prieta. Before dawn on 9 March 1916 several hundred Villistas attacked Columbus, New Mexico, burned part of the town and killed 18 Americans. President Woodrow Wilson sent General John J. Pershing with a force that grew to more than 10,000 men into Chihuahua. Carranza's troops fought Pershing's at Carrizal in June, and the expedition withdrew in February 1917 without finding Villa.",
 why="Villa was angry that the United States had recognised his rival, Venustiano Carranza, and had helped Carranza's army against him. A US intervention would show Carranza as a weak defender of Mexican soil.",
 then="Tension with Mexico made Germany's Zimmermann Telegram of January 1917, offering Mexico its lost territory, all the more alarming when it was published in March. Pershing commanded the American Expeditionary Force in France within months of returning.",
 fact="The expedition was the US Army's first to use aircraft and motor trucks in the field; its eight planes were all wrecked or worn out within weeks."),
"d-zimmermann": dict(
 bluf="German Foreign Minister Arthur Zimmermann wanted Mexico to tie down the United States if it entered the war that Germany's unrestricted submarine campaign was about to provoke. British codebreakers in Room 40 read his telegram offering Mexico Texas, New Mexico and Arizona, and Britain passed it to Washington while hiding how it had been obtained. Published on 1 March 1917, it turned American opinion, and Congress declared war five weeks later.",
 now="It is one of the most consequential intelligence coups in history, and Britain hid how it had broken the code, a model of protecting sources while using what they reveal.",
 story="On 16 January 1917 German Foreign Minister Arthur Zimmermann cabled Germany's minister in Mexico City, proposing an alliance if the United States entered the war and offering Mexico its lost territory in Texas, New Mexico and Arizona. Britain's Room 40 intercepted and decoded it. To hide the source Britain obtained a copy from the Mexico City telegraph office and gave it to US Ambassador Walter Hines Page on 24 February. President Woodrow Wilson released it to the press on 28 February and it was published on 1 March. Zimmermann confirmed it was genuine on 3 March.",
 why="Germany resumed unrestricted submarine warfare on 1 February 1917 and expected the United States to join the war within months. A Mexican front would keep American troops at home.",
 then="With U-boats sinking American ships, Wilson asked Congress for war on 2 April and Congress declared it on 6 April 1917. Mexico declined the offer.",
 fact="Many Americans assumed the telegram was a British forgery until Zimmermann himself confirmed it at a press briefing."),
"na191962b": dict(
 bluf="US President Woodrow Wilson wanted to state Allied war aims that would answer the Bolsheviks' call for a peace without annexations and hold the Allied publics behind the war. On 8 January 1918 he gave Congress fourteen points: open diplomacy, free trade, freedom of the seas, self-determination for Europe's peoples and a League of Nations. Germany sought an armistice on their basis in October 1918, Versailles applied them unevenly, and colonised peoples who had taken up the language of self-determination were refused it.",
 now="Colonised peoples from Egypt to Korea and Vietnam took up Wilson's language of self-determination in 1919, were refused, and turned to other means; the League of Nations and its successor the UN descend from the fourteenth point.",
 story="The Bolsheviks had published the Allies' secret treaties in November 1917 and called for peace without annexations. US President Woodrow Wilson answered in a speech to Congress on 8 January 1918 with fourteen points: open covenants, freedom of the seas, free trade, disarmament, impartial settlement of colonial claims, the evacuation of occupied territory, self-determination for the peoples of Austria-Hungary and the Ottoman Empire, an independent Poland, and a general association of nations. Britain and France accepted them as a basis for armistice in November 1918 with reservations on the seas and on reparations.",
 why="Wilson wanted to define Allied war aims and answer Bolshevik calls for peace after the Russian Revolution. He also wanted a settlement that would avoid the next war.",
 then="Germany asked for an armistice on the basis of the Fourteen Points in October 1918 and held the Treaty of Versailles a betrayal of them. Egyptian, Korean and Vietnamese petitioners were turned away at Paris in 1919, and the Senate rejected the League in November 1919.",
 fact="French Prime Minister Georges Clemenceau is reported to have remarked that God himself had been content with ten commandments."),
"na191962": dict(
 bluf="President Woodrow Wilson wanted the Senate to ratify the Treaty of Versailles and the League of Nations covenant without changes; Republicans under Massachusetts Senator Henry Cabot Lodge demanded reservations to keep Congress's power to decide on war. Wilson refused every reservation, suffered a stroke in October 1919 and ordered his party to vote against Lodge's version. The treaty failed in November 1919 and March 1920, the United States stayed out of the League it had designed, and the League faced the aggressions of the 1930s without it.",
 now="The UN, founded in 1945 with the US at its centre, was designed to avoid the League's mistakes.",
 story="President Woodrow Wilson returned from Paris in July 1919 with a treaty that bound members of the League to defend one another's territory under Article X. Massachusetts Senator Henry Cabot Lodge, chairman of the Foreign Relations Committee, drafted fourteen reservations, chief among them that Congress alone could commit the United States to action. Wilson toured the country for the treaty in September, collapsed and suffered a stroke on 2 October 1919 that left him an invalid. The Senate rejected the treaty with reservations and without them on 19 November 1919, and a final vote on 19 March 1920 fell seven short of two thirds.",
 why="Fear of foreign entanglements, party rivalry after the Republicans won Congress in 1918, and Wilson's refusal to compromise, worsened by his stroke. Lodge and Wilson detested each other.",
 then="The United States made a separate peace with Germany in 1921 and stayed out of the League, which failed to stop Japan in Manchuria in 1931 or Italy in Ethiopia in 1935. The United Nations of 1945 was designed with the United States at its centre and a veto for the great powers.",
 fact="Wilson's wife Edith screened every paper that reached him for the rest of his term, and the extent of his incapacity was hidden from the public."),
}
